# ============================================================================
# IST Flight Reliability Monitor - crontab
#
# EDIT THE PATHS BELOW FIRST - cron needs absolute paths and does not expand ~.
#   sed -i "s|/path/to/ist-flight-monitor|$(pwd)|g" scripts/crontab.example
#
# Install:   crontab scripts/crontab.example
# Inspect:   crontab -l
# Remove:    crontab -r
#
# BEFORE INSTALLING: turn off the in-process scheduler, or every cycle runs
# twice and burns double the provider quota:
#
#     docker compose stop worker
#     # and set SCHEDULER_ENABLED=false in .env
#
# Cron entries are interpreted in CRON_TZ below, which is set to the project's
# operational timezone so "23:00 daily report" means 23:00 in Istanbul.
# ============================================================================

CRON_TZ=Europe/Istanbul
SHELL=/bin/bash
PATH=/usr/local/sbin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/sbin:/usr/bin:/sbin:/bin
# Cron mails stdout/stderr here. Leave blank to disable mail.
MAILTO=""

# --- collection every 8 hours -----------------------------------------------
# Runs at :07 rather than :00 to stay clear of the top-of-hour rush on the
# provider's side. Each run collects, aggregates and dispatches alerts.
#
# Cadence is the lever that pays for extra origins. With two origins and a 24h
# window each cycle costs 4 API calls, so 8-hourly is 12 units/day (~360/month)
# and fits the free tier, where 2-hourly would be ~1,440.
#
# The cost is delay resolution: a flight is observed about 3 times before it
# departs instead of 12, so a delay that grows over an afternoon is seen as a
# couple of steps rather than a curve. Cancellations and final outcomes are
# unaffected. Keep COLLECTION_INTERVAL_MINUTES and STALE_AFTER_MINUTES in .env in
# step with this line - the app refuses to start if staleness is shorter than two
# collection intervals.
7 */8 * * * /path/to/ist-flight-monitor/scripts/collect.sh

# --- daily historical summary to Telegram -----------------------------------
0 23 * * * /path/to/ist-flight-monitor/scripts/daily-report.sh

# --- stall detection --------------------------------------------------------
# Exits non-zero (and so mails MAILTO, if set) when the newest collection run
# is older than two collection intervals, or the last run failed.
#
# Every 4 hours rather than hourly: the threshold is two intervals (16h at the
# current cadence), so an hourly check spawned a container 24 times a day to
# re-answer a question that cannot change that fast.
35 */4 * * * /path/to/ist-flight-monitor/scripts/healthcheck.sh >/dev/null

# ============================================================================
# Quota note for the AeroDataBox free tier (600 units/month):
#   calls per cycle = ceil((LOOKBACK + LOOKAHEAD) / 12)
# With LOOKBACK=3 / LOOKAHEAD=9 that is 1 call/cycle = 24/day = ~25 days.
# To collect more often, change the schedule above to e.g.
#   7 */2 * * *   (every 2 hours -> 48/day -> needs a paid plan with two origins)
# ============================================================================
